  "language_probability": 1,
  "text": "Move the mouse a little. The cursor follows almost instantly. Underneath, an LED lights the surface. A tiny camera takes rapid pictures of the texture. Watch these two snapshots. The dots have not changed. Their positions have shifted. The processor compares the images and measures that shift. If the texture moves left in the images, the mouse must have moved right. That measured movement becomes the cursor's motion. On a plain, glossy surface, there may be too little texture to compare. That is why tracking can fail. An optical mouse does not feel direction. It measures changing pictures.",
  "mean_word_probability": 0.9800550825133616,
